\begin_layout Title
Normalizing Flow
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Predict a Gaussian from Gaussian
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simplest example of predicting a distribution from another distribution is just a Gaussian from a different Gaussian.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Starting from standard normal 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}(z)=\mathcal{N}(0,1)$
\end_inset

 where the target distribution is 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}(x)=\mathcal{N}(10,10)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simplest invertible function to choose is just linear:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
x & =az+b\\
f(z) & =az+b
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This trivially gives a variable that is 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathcal{N}(b,a^{2}).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
this is a bijection function with a non-zero Jacobian so it is valid in this context.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inverted function is just:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
z & =\frac{x-b}{a}\\
f^{-1}(x) & =\frac{x-10}{10}\\
\frac{∂f^{-1}(x)}{∂x} & =\frac{1}{10}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where we'll choose to start with 
\begin_inset Formula $a,b=10,10$
\end_inset

 for simplicity even though any 
\begin_inset Formula $a,b$
\end_inset

 should work.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formulation of the normalizing flow shows that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p_{t}(x) & =p_{0}(z)\det\left|\frac{∂f^{-1}(x)}{∂x}\right|\\
p_{t}(x) & =p_{0}(f^{-1}(x))\det\left|\frac{∂f^{-1}(x)}{∂x}\right|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Placing our specific example details we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p_{t}(x) & =p_{0}\left(\frac{x-10}{10}\right)\cdot\frac{1}{10}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left[\frac{\left(\frac{x-10}{10}\right)^{2}}{2}\right]\cdot\frac{1}{10}\\
p_{t}(x) & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\cdot10}}\exp\left[\frac{\left(x-10\right)^{2}}{2\cdot10}\right]=\mathcal{N}(10,10)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is what we expect.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Practical Use for non-trivial examples 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above formalism could be used easily to find the best fit parameters for the invertible function and therefore the target distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The analogy could be shown through a known and intuitive objective function such as the 
\series bold
KL-Divergence:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{KL}(p_{\text{data}}||p_{t}(x))=\int p_{data}(x)\left[\log\frac{p_{data}(x)}{p_{\theta}(x)}\right]dx
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is essentially the difference between the data distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{data}$
\end_inset

 and the model distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\theta}$
\end_inset

,
 which is described by a general Gaussian distribution above by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{t}(x)=p_{\theta}(x)=p_{0}(f^{-1}(x))\det\left|\frac{∂f^{-1}(x)}{∂x}\right|.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we expand KL-Div:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{KL}(p_{data}||p_{t}(x)) & =\int p_{data}(x)\left[\log\frac{p_{data}(x)}{p_{\theta}(x)}\right]dx=\\
 & =\int p_{data}(x)\left[\log p_{data}(x)-\log p_{\theta}(x)\right]dx\,\,.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Since only the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 are dynamic here,
 the first term doesn't change when taking KL to its minimum,
 so the relevant part is just:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
argmin_{\theta}\left(\text{KL}(p_{data}||p_{t}(x))\right)=-\int p_{data}(x)\log p_{\theta}(x)dx=\mathbb{E}_{p_{data}}\left[-\log p_{\theta}(x)\right]\,\,,
\]

\end_inset

which is the exact same objective function as 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}(x)$
\end_inset

 from above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To get the parameter for the invertible function that connects the latent pdf to the target distirbution one can just minimize the Expectation value of the target pdf - 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}_{p_{data}}\left[-\log\left(p_{\theta}(x)\right)\right]$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Numerical experiment - Steps 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Gather Data...
 - Sample the target distribution - 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{N}(10,10)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Choose an invertible function that meets the conditions - bijection and non-zero Jacobian:
 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{-1}(\theta)=\frac{x-a}{b}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Build the function that needs to be minimized - 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p_{t}(x) & =p_{0}(f^{-1}(x))\det\left|\frac{∂f^{-1}(x)}{∂x}\right|\\
p_{t}(x) & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left[\frac{\left(\frac{x-b}{a}\right)^{2}}{2}\right]\cdot\frac{1}{a}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

and minimize to find the best fit parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}=(a,b)$
\end_inset

,
 which are in this case of course (10,10)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.
 Place the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 in the expression for the distribution:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\\p_{t}(x) & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left[\frac{\left(\frac{x-10}{10}\right)^{2}}{2}\right]\cdot\frac{1}{10}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\cdot10^{2}}}\exp\left[\frac{\left(x-10\right)^{2}}{2\cdot10^{2}}\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

