\begin_layout Section*
Leap Frog VS Euler - Time Propagation Methods 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we want to propagate position and velocity in time what should be done is the following:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z\left(t_{n+1}\right)=z\left(t_{n}\right)+\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n+1}}w(t)dt
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For simplicity what is usually done Euler time propagation is :
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n+1}}w(t)dt\approx\Delta t\bar{w}(t_{n})
\]

\end_inset

The Leap Frog method Consider the integration up to first order instead of order zero:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{w}=\frac{1}{\Delta t}\int_{t_{n}}^{t_{n+1}}w(t)dt=w(t_{n})+\frac{\Delta t}{2}a^{n}+\mathbb{O}\left(\Delta t^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
and then the position at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{n+1}$
\end_inset

 becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z\left(t_{n+1}\right)=z\left(t_{n}\right)+w(t_{n})\Delta t+\frac{\Delta t^{2}}{2}a^{n}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

